-- title:
--   Uniform denominator clearing for sections on a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient
-- statement:
--   The statement is formulated for a complete Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation datum together with an adic frame, and asserts a uniform algebraic clearing of denominators for an arbitrary section near the generic point of the base change to $C$.
--
--   **The local datum.** A prime $r$ is fixed, together with a commutative domain $\mathcal O$ of characteristic zero which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal O$ (`hπ`), completeness of $\mathcal O$ for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), the requirement that the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ have exactly $r$ elements (`hres`), and the requirement that $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`, absolute unramifiedness). A field $K_0$ of characteristic zero is given as a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The unramified datum.** A commutative domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of characteristic zero, an $\mathcal O$-algebra, carrying an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, is subject to: $\pi$-adic completeness (`hOnr_complete`); maximality of the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_max`); every $x \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies some monic $p \in \mathcal O[X]$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`); every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`); and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **The determinant valuation.** A monoid homomorphism $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is given, characterised by `hvdet`: for $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and $n \in \mathbb Z$, $v\!\det(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The group datum.** A group $G$ with a homomorphism $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma \le G$ satisfy: `hcent`, there are $z \in \Gamma$ and $c \in K_0$ with $\sigma z = c \cdot 1$ as a matrix and $v\!\det(\sigma z) = 2$; `hodd`, there is $w \in \Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w) = 1$. A subgroup $\Gamma'$ is pinned by `hΓ'` as the set of $x \in \Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ even. A homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is pinned by `hρ` to be $g \mapsto$ the class of $\sigma g$. On the vertex set of the lattice tree of $(\mathcal O,K_0)$ — homothety classes of full lattices in $K_0^2$ — the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, in the form: there is a finite set $S$ of vertices such that every vertex is carried into $S$ by some element of $\rho(\Gamma')$ (`hcocpt`).
--
--   **The scheme.** A scheme $\mathcal X$ and a morphism $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$ which is proper and flat.
--
--   **The moduli interpretation.** Here `AlgFunctor.corep Onr` is the functor $B \mapsto (O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O\text{-alg}} B)$ on $\mathcal O$-algebras, `Omega K₀ π` is the functor sending $B$ to the set of Deligne data over $B$ (an assignment, to each full lattice $M \subset K_0^2$, of a $B$-submodule $\mathrm{line}(M) \subseteq B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, compatible with lattice inclusions, equivariant for homotheties, and satisfying the non-degeneracy condition at every prime ideal of $B$), and `Scheme.nilpPoints f` is the functor sending $B$ to the set of morphisms $\varphi : \operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X$ with $\varphi$ followed by $f$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $\mathcal O \to B$. A family $\Theta$ is given which, for each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, maps pairs $(\psi, P)$ consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ and a Deligne datum $P$ over $B$ to $B$-points of $f$. It is subject to: naturality in $B$ (`hΘnat`); invariance under the twisted $\Gamma$-action (`hΘinv`), where for $\gamma \in \Gamma$ the relation `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` between $x$ and $x'$ says that $x'_1 = x_1 \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$ and that $x'_2$ is the pullback of $x_2$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$, i.e. $x'_2(\mathrm{line}\,M)$ is the preimage of $x_2(\mathrm{line}\,((\sigma\gamma)^{-1}M))$ under the base-changed lattice isomorphism; the fibre description (`hΘfib`): for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$, every $k$-point of $f$ is of the form $\Theta_k(\psi, P)$, and $\Theta_k(\psi,P) = \Theta_k(\psi,P')$ holds if and only if $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the twisted action of some $\gamma \in \Gamma$; and universality (`hΘuniv`): for every scheme $T$ with a morphism $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ into the nilpotent points of $t$ which is natural and twisted-$\Gamma$-invariant in the same sense, there is a natural family $u$ from the nilpotent points of $f$ to those of $t$ with $u \circ \Theta = \rho'$, and any natural family $u'$ with $u' \circ \Theta = \rho'$ agrees with $u$ on all nilpotent points.
--
--   **The analytic frame.** A field $C$, an algebra over $K_0$, carries a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$; $C$ is complete and algebraically closed. A commutative ring $R$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a $C$-subalgebra source, with scalar towers $\mathcal O \to R \to C$ and $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$. A pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi$ is an element of $K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and whose powers are cofinal for the valuations of nonzero elements of $K_0$ in both directions. The hypothesis `hF` states that $(\pi, \varpi, R)$ is an adic frame: $\pi$ is irreducible; $R \to C$ is injective; the image of $R$ in $C$ is exactly the set of $c$ with $v(c) \le 1$; $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete; the elements $a \in K_0$ with $v(a) \le 1$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$; the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed; and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Further: an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R$; `hrk`, for $x$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ there is $n$ with $v(x)^n \le v(y)$; `hval`, every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates $v(y)$ for some $y \ne 0$; `hex`, every element of the Drinfeld upper half plane $C \setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids $\mathrm{affinoid}(\varpi, n)$; and the ring of $\varpi$-chartwise holomorphic functions on the upper half plane, `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ`, is a domain.
--
--   **The uniformisation.** A map $\Phi$ is given from the set of adic points `AdicPoint K₀ π R` — compatible systems of Deligne data over the rings $R/(\pi^{n+1})$, compatible under the transition maps — to the set of morphisms $p : \operatorname{Spec} R \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathcal O \to R$. The hypothesis `hΦ` has four parts: (i) for every adic point $x$ and every $n$, the reduction of $\Phi(x)$ along $R \to R/(\pi^{n+1})$ equals $\Theta$ evaluated at the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ followed by the quotient map and the $n$-th component $x.\mathrm{pt}\,n$; (ii) $\Phi$ is surjective; (iii) $\Phi(x) = \Phi(x')$ if and only if $x' = x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; (iv) the map sending such an $R$-point $p$ to the $C$-point obtained by composing $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to C$ with $p$ is injective, and every morphism $\operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ arises this way.
--
--   **The base change.** A morphism $s_C : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is pinned by `hsC` to be $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the composite $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$, and the pullback $\mathcal X_C := \mathcal X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O} \operatorname{Spec} C$ is assumed integral, so that it has a generic point $\eta$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every open subset $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing $\eta$ and every section $s \in \Gamma(U, \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C})$ there exist an integer $\ell$ and opens $V_0,\dots,V_{\ell-1}$ of $\mathcal X$ such that:
--
--   1.
--
--   every adic point $x$ admits an index $a$ with $(\Phi x)^{-1}(V_a) = \top$, that is, the preimage of $V_a$ under $\Phi(x) : \operatorname{Spec} R \to \mathcal X$ is all of $\operatorname{Spec} R$; and
--
--   2.
--
--   for each index $a$ there exist an open $W$ of $\mathcal X_C$ with $W \le \mathrm{pr}_1^{-1}(V_a)$ and $\eta \in W$, integers $m, m'$, sections $\mathrm{num}_0,\dots,\mathrm{num}_{m-1} \in \Gamma(V_a, \mathcal O_{\mathcal X})$ with scalars $c^{\mathrm{num}}_i \in C$, and sections $\mathrm{den}_0,\dots,\mathrm{den}_{m'-1} \in \Gamma(V_a, \mathcal O_{\mathcal X})$ with scalars $c^{\mathrm{den}}_j \in C$, such that, writing $D \in \Gamma(W, \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C})$ for $\sum_j$ (restriction to $W$ of the global section of $\mathcal X_C$ obtained from $c^{\mathrm{den}}_j$ through the global sections of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ and the second projection) times (restriction to $W$ of the pullback of $\mathrm{den}_j$ along the first projection), and $N \in \Gamma(W, \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C})$ for the analogous sum built from $c^{\mathrm{num}}_i$ and $\mathrm{num}_i$:
--
--   (a) $D \ne 0$ in $\Gamma(W, \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C})$;
--
--   (b) the product, in $\Gamma(W \sqcap U, \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C})$, of the restriction of $s$ from $U$ and the restriction of $D$ from $W$ equals the restriction of $N$ from $W$ to $W \sqcap U$.
--
--   Thus $s$ agrees, on a neighbourhood of the generic point contained in $\mathrm{pr}_1^{-1}(V_a)$, with the quotient $N/D$ of two $C$-linear combinations of pullbacks of sections defined on $V_a$, the finite family $(V_a)_a$ being chosen uniformly so as to absorb all the points $\Phi(x)$.
--
--   This is the denominator-clearing step for the base change $\mathcal X_C$ of a Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformised curve: near the generic point an arbitrary section is written as a ratio of $C$-combinations of sections pulled back from a finite family of opens of $\mathcal X$ through which all the $R$-points $\Phi(x)$ factor. It feeds the construction of the evaluation map on the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ into the invariant holomorphic functions on the upper half plane, and is cited by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_invariant_chartwiseMeromorphic_pullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_eval_of_smooth`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_invariant_chartwiseMeromorphic_pullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_eval_of_smooth) and [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_invariantFieldOf_eval_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_smooth`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_invariantFieldOf_eval_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient_of_smooth); the local affine input is [`AlgebraicGeometry.isAffineOpen_preimage_fst_and_exists_eq_sum_of_isAffineOpen`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.isAffineOpen_preimage_fst_and_exists_eq_sum_of_isAffineOpen).
